{
  "paraId": "594BDE29",
  "style": "",
  "text": "This study found a clear geographic gradient in the prevalence of cognitive impairment, with higher rates among older people living at higher altitudes. This gradient may partly reflect rurality: people living in mountainous areas contend with daily hardship imposed by the terrain and, in addition, encounter substantial difficulties in accessing care facilities. The aetiology of dementia is also likely to involve substantial socio-environmental contributions, as suggested by documented regional variations in dementia prevalence 41. A systematic review of 51 studies from around the world indicated that people living in rural areas had a higher prevalence of dementia than those in urban areas 41. Comparable findings have been reported among rural-dwelling Thai and Chinese populations, who showed a higher prevalence of dementia symptoms 22, 42. The literature further indicates that LMICs are poorly prepared to care for and support the anticipated future rise in the number of people living with dementia, and that advance care planning in this population remains an unmet need 43. These findings argue for prioritising research in rural, underserved and low-resource settings, both to confirm the association between geographic region and dementia and to clarify the mechanisms that underlie it."
}

